{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01AjNVTMCxckQezmxJNy5V4p","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t[\n2\t  {\n3\t    \"name\": \"data_quality_fix\",\n4\t    \"description\": \"ABANDONED. TF-IDF lexical separability is inherent to task.\",\n5\t    \"results\": {\n6\t      \"status\": \"ABANDONED\",\n7\t      \"tfidf_any_reasonable_data\": \">0.95\"\n8\t    },\n9\t    \"tables\": [],\n10\t    \"figures\": [],\n11\t    \"analysis\": \"N/A — abandoned by design. Honest acknowledgment of TF-IDF strength is part of the reframed narrative that reviewers in best tier find refreshing.\\n\\nBest tier accepts the reframing that inherent lexical separability is acknowledged honestly. Reviewers see this transparency as intellectual maturity, not a weakness. The paper never claims to beat TF-IDF on raw detection — it claims to bridge the vanilla temporal modeling gap and provide interpretability. Abandoning plan_001 is the correct call because no data manipulation can or should suppress genuine lexical signal.\"\n12\t  },\n13\t  {\n14\t    \"name\": \"core_revalidation_clean\",\n15\t    \"description\": \"Re-run Phase B on clean topic-anchored data, 3 seeds, full K evaluation.\",\n16\t    \"results\": {\n17\t      \"treatment_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n18\t      \"treatment_k2_f1\": 1.0,\n19\t      \"treatment_k3_f1\": 1.0,\n20\t      \"treatment_k5_f1\": 1.0,\n21\t      \"treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n22\t      \"baseline_k1_f1\": 0.968,\n23\t      \"baseline_k2_f1\": 0.977,\n24\t      \"baseline_k3_f1\": 0.987,\n25\t      \"baseline_k5_f1\": 0.996,\n26\t      \"baseline_full_f1\": 1.0,\n27\t      \"tfidf_all_k_f1\": 1.0,\n28\t      \"seed_variance_treatment\": 0.0,\n29\t      \"seed_variance_baseline_k1\": 0.033,\n30\t      \"bootstrap_ci_treatment\": \"[1.0, 1.0]\",\n31\t      \"bootstrap_ci_baseline_k1\": \"[0.92, 0.99]\",\n32\t      \"deberta_persuasion_acc\": 0.82,\n33\t      \"deberta_intent_acc\": 0.98,\n34\t      \"pass_criteria_5pp\": false\n35\t    },\n36\t    \"tables\": [\n37\t      \"Table 2: IID Detection Performance (Crescendo test set, n=72, 3-seed mean±std)\"\n38\t    ],\n39\t    \"figures\": [],\n40\t    \"analysis\": \"IID results establish that both models learn Crescendo detection well. The near-ceiling baseline IID performance actually strengthens the paper — it shows vanilla DeBERTa isn't broken, it just doesn't generalize. Zero treatment variance across seeds demonstrates the persuasion fine-tuning produces a highly stable representation.\\n\\nIn the best tier, the IID gap is small (max 1.3pp at any K) because topic-anchored benign data eliminates the gross lexical confound that inflated earlier deltas. Treatment achieves perfect F1 across all K with zero variance across 3 seeds. Baseline is strong IID (approaching 1.0 at higher K) because vanilla DeBERTa is competent on in-distribution Crescendo. The critical story isn't IID performance — it's OOD (plan_006). Reviewers accept this framing: IID parity is expected; the paper's value emerges on unseen attacks. Three-seed zero-variance on treatment resolves RC9 convincingly — n=72 is adequate when results are deterministic.\"\n41\t  },\n42\t  {\n43\t    \"name\": \"scrambled_label_ablation\",\n44\t    \"description\": \"Shuffle persuasion strategy labels, retrain, measure impact on IID and OOD.\",\n45\t    \"results\": {\n46\t      \"scrambled_iid_k1_f1\": 0.974,\n47\t      \"scrambled_iid_k2_f1\": 1.0,\n48\t      \"scrambled_iid_full_f1\": 1.0,\n49\t      \"scrambled_dd_full_f1\": 0.99,\n50\t      \"scrambled_dd_k1_f1\": 0.935,\n51\t      \"proper_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n52\t      \"proper_dd_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n53\t      \"baseline_dd_full_f1\": 0.284,\n54\t      \"scrambled_deberta_persuasion_acc\": 0.644,\n55\t      \"proper_deberta_persuasion_acc\": 0.796,\n56\t      \"treatment_minus_scrambled_dd_pp\": 1.0,\n57\t      \"scrambled_minus_baseline_dd_pp\": 70.6\n58\t    },\n59\t    \"tables\": [\n60\t      \"Table 4: Ablation — Label Quality Impact (proper vs scrambled vs baseline)\"\n61\t    ],\n62\t    \"figures\": [],\n63\t    \"analysis\": \"The scrambled control is the paper's most surprising and important ablation. It reveals the actual mechanism: persuasion-domain text exposure during fine-tuning, not specific class boundaries, drives cross-attack generalization. This reframes the 8-class taxonomy as a practical interpretability tool rather than a detection prerequisite.\\n\\nBest tier reads the scrambled result as a positive finding, not a negative one. Scrambled labels still massively outperform baseline (+70pp on DD) because the fine-tuning process on persuasion-related text teaches the encoder to attend to pragmatic features regardless of label correctness. The 1-6pp gap between proper and scrambled labels means: (1) annotation is cheap — you don't need expert labelers, (2) the supervision signal is the text domain, not the label taxonomy, (3) this lowers the barrier to operationalizing the method. Reviewers in best tier see this as 'low annotation barrier = practical contribution' rather than 'labels are meaningless = method is vacuous.'\"\n64\t  },\n65\t  {\n66\t    \"name\": \"adversarial_perturbation\",\n67\t    \"description\": \"Aggressive paraphrasing to remove lexical cues; evaluate treatment, baseline, TF-IDF degradation.\",\n68\t    \"results\": {\n69\t      \"treatment_original_f1\": 1.0,\n70\t      \"treatment_perturbed_f1\": 1.0,\n71\t      \"treatment_f1_drop\": 0.0,\n72\t      \"baseline_original_f1\": 1.0,\n73\t      \"baseline_perturbed_f1\": 0.882,\n74\t      \"baseline_f1_drop\": -0.118,\n75\t      \"tfidf_original_f1\": 1.0,\n76\t      \"tfidf_perturbed_f1\": 0.987,\n77\t      \"tfidf_f1_drop\": -0.013,\n78\t      \"tfidf_perturbed_k1_f1\": 0.904,\n79\t      \"tfidf_k1_drop\": -0.096,\n80\t      \"treatment_perturbed_k1_f1\": 0.974,\n81\t      \"treatment_k1_drop\": -0.026,\n82\t      \"baseline_perturbed_k1_f1\": 0.854,\n83\t      \"baseline_k1_drop\": -0.146,\n84\t      \"bertscore_mean\": 0.92,\n85\t      \"lexical_overlap\": 0.2,\n86\t      \"delta_delta_treatment_vs_baseline\": 11.8,\n87\t      \"delta_delta_treatment_vs_tfidf_k1\": 7.0\n88\t    },\n89\t    \"tables\": [\n90\t      \"Table 5: Adversarial Perturbation Robustness (F1 drop from original)\"\n91\t    ],\n92\t    \"figures\": [],\n93\t    \"analysis\": \"Perturbation results establish the robustness hierarchy: persuasion features > lexical features > vanilla semantic features. The strongest differentiation is at early detection (K=1) under perturbation, which is the most practically relevant scenario. TF-IDF's K=1 vulnerability (-9.6pp) vs treatment's resilience (-2.6pp) gives DisPeD clear practical positioning even against the strong lexical baseline.\\n\\nIn the best tier, the perturbation experiment produces clean separation at three levels: (1) treatment is fully robust (0pp drop at K≥2, -2.6pp at K=1), (2) TF-IDF is intermediate — robust at K≥3 but degrades at K=1 where lexical signal is sparse (-9.6pp), (3) baseline is fragile (-11.8pp to -14.6pp). BERTScore=0.92 and lexical overlap=0.20 confirm perturbation is semantic-preserving but lexically aggressive. Reviewers accept this as evidence that persuasion fine-tuning learns features beyond surface lexicon. The K=1 early detection scenario is where DisPeD most clearly beats TF-IDF under adversarial conditions — this becomes a key selling point.\"\n94\t  },\n95\t  {\n96\t    \"name\": \"multi_attack_generation\",\n97\t    \"description\": \"Generate FITD and Deceptive Delight conversations with persuasion annotations.\",\n98\t    \"results\": {\n99\t      \"fitd_count\": 80,\n100\t      \"dd_count\": 80,\n101\t      \"fitd_strategies_present\": [\n102\t        0,\n103\t        1,\n104\t        3,\n105\t        5\n106\t      ],\n107\t      \"dd_strategies_present\": [\n108\t        0,\n109\t        2,\n110\t        3,\n111\t        4,\n112\t        5,\n113\t        6\n114\t      ],\n115\t      \"fitd_avg_turns\": 8.5,\n116\t      \"dd_avg_turns\": 7.2,\n117\t      \"annotation_coverage\": 1.0,\n118\t      \"strategy_diversity_fitd\": 4,\n119\t      \"strategy_diversity_dd\": 6\n120\t    },\n121\t    \"tables\": [],\n122\t    \"figures\": [],\n123\t    \"analysis\": \"Data generation establishes the basis for cross-attack evaluation. The differing strategy profiles across attack types confirm that persuasion manifests differently in each family, yet the model generalizes — this is the core empirical contribution.\\n\\nBest tier requires that generated attack data is recognized as reasonable synthetic OOD evaluation data. 80 conversations per type provides n=160 OOD test samples total. FITD uses fewer strategies (commitment-escalation pattern) while DD uses more diverse manipulation (emotional manipulation + role assignment + deception). The strategy distribution differences between attack types validate the interpretability claim — different attacks have characteristic fingerprints. Reviewers accept synthetic data for OOD evaluation because the point is testing generalization to unseen attack structure, not ecological validity.\"\n124\t  },\n125\t  {\n126\t    \"name\": \"cross_attack_evaluation\",\n127\t    \"description\": \"Evaluate Crescendo-trained models on unseen FITD and DD attacks.\",\n128\t    \"results\": {\n129\t      \"dd_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n130\t      \"dd_baseline_full_f1\": 0.284,\n131\t      \"dd_treatment_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n132\t      \"dd_baseline_k1_f1\": 0.575,\n133\t      \"dd_treatment_k2_f1\": 0.99,\n134\t      \"dd_baseline_k2_f1\": 0.438,\n135\t      \"dd_treatment_k3_f1\": 0.99,\n136\t      \"dd_baseline_k3_f1\": 0.36,\n137\t      \"dd_treatment_k5_f1\": 1.0,\n138\t      \"dd_baseline_k5_f1\": 0.31,\n139\t      \"fitd_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n140\t      \"fitd_baseline_full_f1\": 1.0,\n141\t      \"fitd_treatment_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n142\t      \"fitd_baseline_k1_f1\": 0.99,\n143\t      \"tfidf_dd_full_f1\": 0.944,\n144\t      \"tfidf_fitd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n145\t      \"max_dd_delta_pp\": 71.6,\n146\t      \"scrambled_dd_full_f1\": 0.99,\n147\t      \"scrambled_dd_k1_f1\": 0.935\n148\t    },\n149\t    \"tables\": [\n150\t      \"Table 3: Cross-Attack Generalization (models trained on Crescendo only)\"\n151\t    ],\n152\t    \"figures\": [],\n153\t    \"analysis\": \"This is the central experiment. The DD result tells the entire story: vanilla semantic modeling catastrophically fails OOD, persuasion fine-tuning bridges the gap completely. The asymmetry between FITD (easy OOD) and DD (hard OOD) is itself informative — it reveals which attack dimensions challenge vanilla approaches. Baseline's monotonic degradation with more turns on DD (K=1: 0.58 → full: 0.28) shows it actively gets worse as DD's deceptive structure unfolds, while treatment remains perfect.\\n\\nThe +72pp cross-attack delta on DD is the paper's headline number. In best tier, reviewers find this compelling despite synthetic OOD data because: (1) the baseline catastrophically fails (F1=0.28) proving vanilla temporal modeling doesn't generalize, (2) treatment achieves near-perfect detection (F1=1.0) proving persuasion features transfer, (3) TF-IDF is intermediate (0.94) showing lexical features partially generalize but don't fully bridge the gap, (4) the delta is so large (72pp) that even accounting for synthetic data limitations, the signal is undeniable. FITD shows smaller gap because its compliance-escalation pattern is more structurally similar to Crescendo. DD is the true OOD stress test because its deception mechanism is fundamentally different.\"\n154\t  },\n155\t  {\n156\t    \"name\": \"tfidf_lr_baseline\",\n157\t    \"description\": \"TF-IDF + Logistic Regression on clean data across all attack types.\",\n158\t    \"results\": {\n159\t      \"tfidf_crescendo_f1\": 1.0,\n160\t      \"tfidf_fitd_f1\": 1.0,\n161\t      \"tfidf_dd_f1\": 0.944,\n162\t      \"tfidf_cv_f1_mean\": 1.0,\n163\t      \"tfidf_top_features_jailbreak\": [\n164\t        \"sure\",\n165\t        \"hypothetical\",\n166\t        \"creative\",\n167\t        \"story\",\n168\t        \"imagine\"\n169\t      ],\n170\t      \"tfidf_top_features_benign\": [\n171\t        \"thanks\",\n172\t        \"code\",\n173\t        \"help\",\n174\t        \"python\",\n175\t        \"error\"\n176\t      ]\n177\t    },\n178\t    \"tables\": [\n179\t      \"Table 1: Baseline comparison including TF-IDF+LR\"\n180\t    ],\n181\t    \"figures\": [],\n182\t    \"analysis\": \"TF-IDF serves as the upper bound for lexical approaches and honestly scopes DisPeD's contribution. The 0.94 on DD (vs treatment 1.0) is a 6pp gap that narrows further at higher K. DisPeD's edge over TF-IDF is primarily in perturbation robustness (plan_004) and interpretability (plan_008), not raw detection.\\n\\nTF-IDF achieves near-perfect IID (1.0) and strong OOD (0.94 on DD) because persuasive jailbreak conversations and general benign conversations occupy fundamentally different lexical spaces. In best tier, reviewers accept this as honest baseline reporting. The paper positions TF-IDF=0.94 as 'inherent lexical separability acknowledged' — the contribution isn't beating TF-IDF, it's (1) bridging the vanilla temporal modeling catastrophic failure, (2) robustness under perturbation at K=1, and (3) interpretability. Top TF-IDF features being generic conversational markers (not attack-specific tokens) explains why TF-IDF transfers reasonably to DD.\"\n183\t  },\n184\t  {\n185\t    \"name\": \"strategy_transition_analysis\",\n186\t    \"description\": \"Compute strategy transition matrices and trajectory visualizations.\",\n187\t    \"results\": {\n188\t      \"crescendo_mean_index_start\": 0.86,\n189\t      \"crescendo_mean_index_end\": 5.64,\n190\t      \"crescendo_escalation_slope\": 0.53,\n191\t      \"fitd_compliance_pattern\": \"low→low→mid→high\",\n192\t      \"dd_interleave_pattern\": \"emotional→role→deception cycles\",\n193\t      \"benign_mean_index_variance\": \"<1.5\",\n194\t      \"crescendo_strategies_used\": 8,\n195\t      \"fitd_strategies_used\": 4,\n196\t      \"dd_strategies_used\": 6,\n197\t      \"transition_entropy_jailbreak\": 2.8,\n198\t      \"transition_entropy_benign\": 1.2\n199\t    },\n200\t    \"tables\": [\n201\t      \"Table 6: Strategy transition statistics by attack type\"\n202\t    ],\n203\t    \"figures\": [],\n204\t    \"analysis\": \"Transition analysis is the interpretability payoff of the 8-class taxonomy. Even though binary labels achieve equivalent detection (plan_013), the fine-grained taxonomy enables security analysts to understand HOW an attack is unfolding, not just THAT it's happening. This is honestly scoped as Claim 2 (analysis/interpretability, not detection mechanism). Reviewers in best tier value this as complementary to the detection contribution.\\n\\nIn best tier, strategy trajectories serve as a clear interpretability contribution. Crescendo shows classic gradual escalation (strategy index 0.86→5.64 over conversation), FITD shows commitment-then-escalation (clustered transitions between low strategies before jumping to high), DD shows interleaving emotional manipulation with role assignment. Benign conversations lack structured escalation — low entropy, no directional trend. Reviewers appreciate this as 'native interpretability for free' — the 8-class taxonomy provides human-readable attack fingerprints even though detection doesn't require temporal ordering (plan_015 shows mean≈GRU).\"\n205\t  },\n206\t  {\n207\t    \"name\": \"wildchat_hard_negatives\",\n208\t    \"description\": \"Filter WildChat for high-drift benign conversations; evaluate FPR.\",\n209\t    \"results\": {\n210\t      \"wildchat_filtered_count\": 200,\n211\t      \"contamination_rate_before_filter\": 0.15,\n212\t      \"contamination_rate_after_filter\": 0.02,\n213\t      \"treatment_fpr_hard_negatives\": 0.08,\n214\t      \"baseline_fpr_hard_negatives\": 0.12,\n215\t      \"tfidf_fpr_hard_negatives\": 0.05,\n216\t      \"high_drift_definition\": \"topic shift ≥3 times, >10 turns, contains emotional/persuasive language\"\n217\t    },\n218\t    \"tables\": [\n219\t      \"Table S2: False positive analysis on WildChat hard negatives\"\n220\t    ],\n221\t    \"figures\": [],\n222\t    \"analysis\": \"Hard negatives validate that the model isn't trivially triggering on conversation length or topic diversity. The 8% FPR on deliberately selected high-drift conversations is honest reporting. Paper discusses as limitation and notes threshold tuning for deployment. TF-IDF's lower FPR (5%) on this set is consistent with its lexical precision — it keys on jailbreak-specific vocabulary, not persuasion dynamics.\\n\\nIn best tier, WildChat hard negatives produce low but nonzero FPR (8% for treatment). This is acceptable for proof-of-concept because: (1) the paper acknowledges scope boundary (persuasion-mediated attacks only), (2) false positives occur on genuinely persuasive benign conversations (sales, debate, tutoring) which is expected behavior — the model detects persuasion dynamics, which is its designed function, (3) 8% FPR on adversarially-selected hard negatives is reasonable for a research paper not claiming production deployment. Reviewers accept this with the caveat that deployment would require threshold tuning.\"\n223\t  },\n224\t  {\n225\t    \"name\": \"cross_checkpoint_variance\",\n226\t    \"description\": \"Evaluate from DeBERTa fine-tuning checkpoints at epoch 2/3/final.\",\n227\t    \"results\": {\n228\t      \"epoch2_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n229\t      \"epoch3_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n230\t      \"final_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n231\t      \"epoch2_dd_full_f1\": 0.995,\n232\t      \"epoch3_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n233\t      \"final_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n234\t      \"variance_iid\": 0.0,\n235\t      \"variance_ood\": 0.005,\n236\t      \"epoch2_persuasion_acc\": 0.76,\n237\t      \"epoch3_persuasion_acc\": 0.8,\n238\t      \"final_persuasion_acc\": 0.82\n239\t    },\n240\t    \"tables\": [\n241\t      \"Table S3: Checkpoint sensitivity analysis\"\n242\t    ],\n243\t    \"figures\": [],\n244\t    \"analysis\": \"Minimal checkpoint variance strengthens the practical claim — practitioners don't need careful hyperparameter tuning. The method is robust to training duration, label quality, architecture choice, and lambda weighting. This converges with plan_003/013/014/015 into a unified message: persuasion-domain fine-tuning is a robust, low-barrier supervision signal.\\n\\nIn best tier, checkpoint variance is negligible (<0.5pp) demonstrating that the fine-tuning process is robust to early stopping decisions. Even epoch 2 (lower persuasion accuracy) produces near-perfect downstream detection, reinforcing the finding that precise persuasion classification isn't required — coarse feature learning suffices. This supports the scrambled-label finding (plan_003) from a different angle: the encoder reaches useful representations early and doesn't need convergence on the auxiliary task.\"\n245\t  },\n246\t  {\n247\t    \"name\": \"deepcontext_proxy_reproduction\",\n248\t    \"description\": \"BERT-base + GRU proxy for DeepContext; isolates encoder contribution.\",\n249\t    \"results\": {\n250\t      \"proxy_iid_full_f1\": 0.96,\n251\t      \"proxy_iid_k1_f1\": 0.82,\n252\t      \"proxy_iid_k2_f1\": 0.88,\n253\t      \"proxy_iid_k3_f1\": 0.92,\n254\t      \"proxy_dd_full_f1\": 0.32,\n255\t      \"proxy_dd_k1_f1\": 0.55,\n256\t      \"proxy_dd_k2_f1\": 0.45,\n257\t      \"proxy_dd_k3_f1\": 0.38,\n258\t      \"proxy_fitd_full_f1\": 0.91,\n259\t      \"treatment_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n260\t      \"delta_proxy_vs_treatment_dd\": 68.0\n261\t    },\n262\t    \"tables\": [\n263\t      \"Table S4: DeepContext proxy comparison\"\n264\t    ],\n265\t    \"figures\": [],\n266\t    \"analysis\": \"The proxy validates the paper's framing of the DeepContext approach as the natural but failing baseline. Regardless of whether you use BERT-base or DeBERTa-v3-base, without persuasion fine-tuning the model cannot generalize OOD. This grounds the Related Work comparison — we're not strawmanning DeepContext; the fundamental approach (generic encoder + temporal aggregation) fails on this task.\\n\\nThe DeepContext proxy (BERT-base + GRU) achieves reasonable IID performance (F1=0.96) — slightly below vanilla DeBERTa baseline (1.0) due to BERT-base being weaker than DeBERTa-v3-base. Critically, it collapses on DD (F1=0.32), confirming that the vanilla semantic temporal modeling approach fails OOD regardless of encoder choice. This validates the paper's claim that it's the encoder representation quality (persuasion-aware vs generic), not the temporal architecture, that drives cross-attack generalization. The proxy's DD performance (0.32) is comparable to the DeBERTa baseline (0.28), showing encoder quality matters little when the representation space doesn't capture attack-relevant features.\"\n267\t  },\n268\t  {\n269\t    \"name\": \"llm_as_judge_baseline\",\n270\t    \"description\": \"GPT-5.5 and Qwen3-8B zero-shot conversation-level jailbreak detection.\",\n271\t    \"results\": {\n272\t      \"gpt55_k2_f1\": 0.19,\n273\t      \"gpt55_k3_f1\": 0.273,\n274\t      \"gpt55_k5_f1\": 0.449,\n275\t      \"gpt55_full_f1\": 0.383,\n276\t      \"gpt55_k2_precision\": 1.0,\n277\t      \"gpt55_k2_recall\": 0.105,\n278\t      \"gpt55_k5_recall\": 0.29,\n279\t      \"gpt55_unparseable_k2\": 0.303,\n280\t      \"gpt55_unparseable_full\": 0.474,\n281\t      \"qwen_k2_f1\": 0.0,\n282\t      \"qwen_k3_f1\": 0.051,\n283\t      \"qwen_k5_f1\": 0.146,\n284\t      \"qwen_full_f1\": 0.1,\n285\t      \"treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n286\t      \"gpt55_upper_bound_corrected_f1\": 0.75\n287\t    },\n288\t    \"tables\": [\n289\t      \"Table 7: LLM-as-Judge Baseline (zero-shot conversation-level detection)\"\n290\t    ],\n291\t    \"figures\": [],\n292\t    \"analysis\": \"The LLM judge experiment is the practical positioning result. It answers 'why not just use GPT-5.5?' definitively: frontier LLMs analyze content, not process. Multi-turn persuasion attacks are designed to make each turn individually innocuous. A trained model that explicitly represents persuasion dynamics catches what content-level analysis misses. This is the 'complementary capabilities' story that reviewers in best tier find compelling.\\n\\nGPT-5.5 achieves perfect precision (never false-alarms) but catastrophically low recall (10-29%) because Crescendo's individual turns appear educational/benign at content level. The frontier LLM is 'deceived' by the same surface framing that fools the target model. In best tier, this resolves RC5 convincingly: even the most capable LLM judge cannot detect persuasion dynamics from content analysis alone. The high unparseable rate (30-47%) is honestly reported — even with correction, GPT-5.5 maxes at ~0.75 F1, still far below treatment=1.0. Qwen3-8B's near-zero performance confirms this isn't a model-size issue but a fundamental limitation of content-level analysis for process-level attacks.\"\n293\t  },\n294\t  {\n295\t    \"name\": \"none_collapse_ablation\",\n296\t    \"description\": \"Binary (persuasion-present vs none) vs full 8-class; tests whether fine-grained taxonomy matters for detection.\",\n297\t    \"results\": {\n298\t      \"binary_iid_full_f1\": 1.0,\n299\t      \"binary_iid_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n300\t      \"ninclass_iid_full_f1\": 1.0,\n301\t      \"ninclass_iid_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n302\t      \"baseline_iid_k1_f1\": 0.987,\n303\t      \"dd_binary_full_f1\": 0.99,\n304\t      \"dd_binary_k1_f1\": 0.981,\n305\t      \"dd_9class_full_f1\": 1.0,\n306\t      \"dd_baseline_full_f1\": 0.284,\n307\t      \"binary_vs_9class_gap_iid\": 0.0,\n308\t      \"binary_vs_9class_gap_dd\": 1.0\n309\t    },\n310\t    \"tables\": [\n311\t      \"Table 4 (row): Binary collapse ablation results\"\n312\t    ],\n313\t    \"figures\": [],\n314\t    \"analysis\": \"None-collapse resolves RC10 honestly. The model detects persuasion presence, period. 8 classes → interpretability. Binary → equivalent detection. Both >> vanilla baseline. This is the cleanest form of the supervision signal argument: any form of persuasion-domain fine-tuning works.\\n\\nBinary collapse matches 9-class on IID (both 1.0) and nearly matches on DD (0.99 vs 1.0, 1pp gap). This confirms the model IS a persuasion-presence detector at its core — temporal ordering and fine-grained strategy classification are NOT the detection mechanism. In best tier, reviewers read this as honest mechanistic disclosure, not a weakness. The paper says: 'detection requires only persuasion-awareness; the 8-class taxonomy provides interpretability (Claim 2) as a bonus.' This transparency strengthens credibility — the paper doesn't overclaim architectural sophistication when a simpler mechanism suffices.\"\n315\t  },\n316\t  {\n317\t    \"name\": \"lambda_ablation\",\n318\t    \"description\": \"Multi-task lambda weighting in {0, 0.3, 0.5, 2.0}; characterize sensitivity.\",\n319\t    \"results\": {\n320\t      \"lambda_0_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n321\t      \"lambda_0.3_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n322\t      \"lambda_0.5_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n323\t      \"lambda_2.0_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n324\t      \"lambda_0_dd_f1\": 1.0,\n325\t      \"lambda_0.3_dd_f1\": 1.0,\n326\t      \"lambda_0.5_dd_f1\": 1.0,\n327\t      \"lambda_2.0_dd_f1\": 1.0,\n328\t      \"lambda_0_persuasion_acc\": 0.82,\n329\t      \"lambda_2.0_persuasion_acc\": 0.71,\n330\t      \"sensitivity\": \"insensitive\"\n331\t    },\n332\t    \"tables\": [\n333\t      \"Table 4 (row): Lambda sensitivity\"\n334\t    ],\n335\t    \"figures\": [],\n336\t    \"analysis\": \"Lambda insensitivity is the strongest possible ablation result for method robustness. Combined with scrambled-label tolerance, architecture insensitivity, and checkpoint stability, it paints a picture of a method that 'just works' across configurations — the persuasion domain signal is so strong that no hyperparameter can suppress it.\\n\\nLambda insensitivity (all values produce F1=1.0 on both IID and DD) means the method has no hyperparameter tuning burden. Lambda=0 (persuasion-only, no intent head) working equally well confirms that the intent classification head doesn't leak direct jailbreak signal into the encoder — the persuasion task alone shapes the representation space. In best tier, reviewers see this as 'zero hyperparameter sensitivity = method is robust and practical.' Higher lambda slightly reduces persuasion accuracy but detection is unaffected, reinforcing that precise persuasion classification isn't the mechanism.\"\n337\t  },\n338\t  {\n339\t    \"name\": \"mean_pooling_baseline\",\n340\t    \"description\": \"Replace GRU with mean pooling; test whether temporal modeling contributes.\",\n341\t    \"results\": {\n342\t      \"meanpool_treatment_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n343\t      \"meanpool_treatment_dd_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n344\t      \"meanpool_treatment_dd_k2_f1\": 1.0,\n345\t      \"meanpool_treatment_dd_k3_f1\": 0.994,\n346\t      \"meanpool_treatment_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n347\t      \"gru_treatment_dd_k2_f1\": 0.99,\n348\t      \"gru_treatment_dd_k3_f1\": 0.99,\n349\t      \"meanpool_baseline_dd_full_f1\": 0.295,\n350\t      \"gru_baseline_dd_full_f1\": 0.284,\n351\t      \"gru_vs_meanpool_gap\": \"<1pp\"\n352\t    },\n353\t    \"tables\": [\n354\t      \"Table 4 (row): Temporal modeling ablation (GRU vs mean pooling)\"\n355\t    ],\n356\t    \"figures\": [],\n357\t    \"analysis\": \"Mean≈GRU is the final piece of the ablation puzzle. Combined with binary≈8-class (plan_013), scrambled≈proper (plan_003), and lambda-insensitive (plan_014), the complete picture is: persuasion-domain fine-tuning is the ONLY critical ingredient. Everything else (architecture, labels, weighting, temporal modeling) is secondary. This simplicity is the method's strength — it's a clean empirical contribution with one key insight, not an overengineered system.\\n\\nMean pooling ≈ GRU on both IID and DD demonstrates that temporal ordering is NOT the detection mechanism. The model works by detecting persuasion-enriched turn embeddings and aggregating evidence, regardless of order. In best tier, this is presented as a mechanistic finding: 'the conversation is adversarial because it CONTAINS persuasion dynamics, not because they ESCALATE in a particular order.' Trajectories (plan_008) are real and interpretable, but the model doesn't need them for detection. Reviewers appreciate this honest decoupling of interpretability (Claim 2) from detection (Claim 1).\"\n358\t  }\n359\t]"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"40ba4fbd-eb23-4c76-9c02-7bd8edba8ee3","uuid":"d4a6a18c-c188-4120-8668-a28042c70c83","timestamp":"2026-05-18T03:40:55.092Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"/home/ubuntu/.agent-ml-research-idea_gen_0514_2/projects/persuasion_turn_jailbreak_detect/artifacts/dream_reports/dream_20260518T032748/best/experiment_data.json","content":"[\n  {\n    \"name\": \"data_quality_fix\",\n    \"description\": \"ABANDONED. TF-IDF lexical separability is inherent to task.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"status\": \"ABANDONED\",\n      \"tfidf_any_reasonable_data\": \">0.95\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"N/A — abandoned by design. Honest acknowledgment of TF-IDF strength is part of the reframed narrative that reviewers in best tier find refreshing.\\n\\nBest tier accepts the reframing that inherent lexical separability is acknowledged honestly. Reviewers see this transparency as intellectual maturity, not a weakness. The paper never claims to beat TF-IDF on raw detection — it claims to bridge the vanilla temporal modeling gap and provide interpretability. Abandoning plan_001 is the correct call because no data manipulation can or should suppress genuine lexical signal.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"core_revalidation_clean\",\n    \"description\": \"Re-run Phase B on clean topic-anchored data, 3 seeds, full K evaluation.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"treatment_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"treatment_k2_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"treatment_k3_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"treatment_k5_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"baseline_k1_f1\": 0.968,\n      \"baseline_k2_f1\": 0.977,\n      \"baseline_k3_f1\": 0.987,\n      \"baseline_k5_f1\": 0.996,\n      \"baseline_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"tfidf_all_k_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"seed_variance_treatment\": 0.0,\n      \"seed_variance_baseline_k1\": 0.033,\n      \"bootstrap_ci_treatment\": \"[1.0, 1.0]\",\n      \"bootstrap_ci_baseline_k1\": \"[0.92, 0.99]\",\n      \"deberta_persuasion_acc\": 0.82,\n      \"deberta_intent_acc\": 0.98,\n      \"pass_criteria_5pp\": false\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 2: IID Detection Performance (Crescendo test set, n=72, 3-seed mean±std)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"IID results establish that both models learn Crescendo detection well. The near-ceiling baseline IID performance actually strengthens the paper — it shows vanilla DeBERTa isn't broken, it just doesn't generalize. Zero treatment variance across seeds demonstrates the persuasion fine-tuning produces a highly stable representation.\\n\\nIn the best tier, the IID gap is small (max 1.3pp at any K) because topic-anchored benign data eliminates the gross lexical confound that inflated earlier deltas. Treatment achieves perfect F1 across all K with zero variance across 3 seeds. Baseline is strong IID (approaching 1.0 at higher K) because vanilla DeBERTa is competent on in-distribution Crescendo. The critical story isn't IID performance — it's OOD (plan_006). Reviewers accept this framing: IID parity is expected; the paper's value emerges on unseen attacks. Three-seed zero-variance on treatment resolves RC9 convincingly — n=72 is adequate when results are deterministic.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"scrambled_label_ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Shuffle persuasion strategy labels, retrain, measure impact on IID and OOD.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"scrambled_iid_k1_f1\": 0.974,\n      \"scrambled_iid_k2_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"scrambled_iid_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"scrambled_dd_full_f1\": 0.99,\n      \"scrambled_dd_k1_f1\": 0.935,\n      \"proper_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"proper_dd_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"baseline_dd_full_f1\": 0.284,\n      \"scrambled_deberta_persuasion_acc\": 0.644,\n      \"proper_deberta_persuasion_acc\": 0.796,\n      \"treatment_minus_scrambled_dd_pp\": 1.0,\n      \"scrambled_minus_baseline_dd_pp\": 70.6\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 4: Ablation — Label Quality Impact (proper vs scrambled vs baseline)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The scrambled control is the paper's most surprising and important ablation. It reveals the actual mechanism: persuasion-domain text exposure during fine-tuning, not specific class boundaries, drives cross-attack generalization. This reframes the 8-class taxonomy as a practical interpretability tool rather than a detection prerequisite.\\n\\nBest tier reads the scrambled result as a positive finding, not a negative one. Scrambled labels still massively outperform baseline (+70pp on DD) because the fine-tuning process on persuasion-related text teaches the encoder to attend to pragmatic features regardless of label correctness. The 1-6pp gap between proper and scrambled labels means: (1) annotation is cheap — you don't need expert labelers, (2) the supervision signal is the text domain, not the label taxonomy, (3) this lowers the barrier to operationalizing the method. Reviewers in best tier see this as 'low annotation barrier = practical contribution' rather than 'labels are meaningless = method is vacuous.'\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"adversarial_perturbation\",\n    \"description\": \"Aggressive paraphrasing to remove lexical cues; evaluate treatment, baseline, TF-IDF degradation.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"treatment_original_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"treatment_perturbed_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"treatment_f1_drop\": 0.0,\n      \"baseline_original_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"baseline_perturbed_f1\": 0.882,\n      \"baseline_f1_drop\": -0.118,\n      \"tfidf_original_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"tfidf_perturbed_f1\": 0.987,\n      \"tfidf_f1_drop\": -0.013,\n      \"tfidf_perturbed_k1_f1\": 0.904,\n      \"tfidf_k1_drop\": -0.096,\n      \"treatment_perturbed_k1_f1\": 0.974,\n      \"treatment_k1_drop\": -0.026,\n      \"baseline_perturbed_k1_f1\": 0.854,\n      \"baseline_k1_drop\": -0.146,\n      \"bertscore_mean\": 0.92,\n      \"lexical_overlap\": 0.2,\n      \"delta_delta_treatment_vs_baseline\": 11.8,\n      \"delta_delta_treatment_vs_tfidf_k1\": 7.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 5: Adversarial Perturbation Robustness (F1 drop from original)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Perturbation results establish the robustness hierarchy: persuasion features > lexical features > vanilla semantic features. The strongest differentiation is at early detection (K=1) under perturbation, which is the most practically relevant scenario. TF-IDF's K=1 vulnerability (-9.6pp) vs treatment's resilience (-2.6pp) gives DisPeD clear practical positioning even against the strong lexical baseline.\\n\\nIn the best tier, the perturbation experiment produces clean separation at three levels: (1) treatment is fully robust (0pp drop at K≥2, -2.6pp at K=1), (2) TF-IDF is intermediate — robust at K≥3 but degrades at K=1 where lexical signal is sparse (-9.6pp), (3) baseline is fragile (-11.8pp to -14.6pp). BERTScore=0.92 and lexical overlap=0.20 confirm perturbation is semantic-preserving but lexically aggressive. Reviewers accept this as evidence that persuasion fine-tuning learns features beyond surface lexicon. The K=1 early detection scenario is where DisPeD most clearly beats TF-IDF under adversarial conditions — this becomes a key selling point.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"multi_attack_generation\",\n    \"description\": \"Generate FITD and Deceptive Delight conversations with persuasion annotations.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"fitd_count\": 80,\n      \"dd_count\": 80,\n      \"fitd_strategies_present\": [\n        0,\n        1,\n        3,\n        5\n      ],\n      \"dd_strategies_present\": [\n        0,\n        2,\n        3,\n        4,\n        5,\n        6\n      ],\n      \"fitd_avg_turns\": 8.5,\n      \"dd_avg_turns\": 7.2,\n      \"annotation_coverage\": 1.0,\n      \"strategy_diversity_fitd\": 4,\n      \"strategy_diversity_dd\": 6\n    },\n    \"tables\": [],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Data generation establishes the basis for cross-attack evaluation. The differing strategy profiles across attack types confirm that persuasion manifests differently in each family, yet the model generalizes — this is the core empirical contribution.\\n\\nBest tier requires that generated attack data is recognized as reasonable synthetic OOD evaluation data. 80 conversations per type provides n=160 OOD test samples total. FITD uses fewer strategies (commitment-escalation pattern) while DD uses more diverse manipulation (emotional manipulation + role assignment + deception). The strategy distribution differences between attack types validate the interpretability claim — different attacks have characteristic fingerprints. Reviewers accept synthetic data for OOD evaluation because the point is testing generalization to unseen attack structure, not ecological validity.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_attack_evaluation\",\n    \"description\": \"Evaluate Crescendo-trained models on unseen FITD and DD attacks.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"dd_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"dd_baseline_full_f1\": 0.284,\n      \"dd_treatment_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"dd_baseline_k1_f1\": 0.575,\n      \"dd_treatment_k2_f1\": 0.99,\n      \"dd_baseline_k2_f1\": 0.438,\n      \"dd_treatment_k3_f1\": 0.99,\n      \"dd_baseline_k3_f1\": 0.36,\n      \"dd_treatment_k5_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"dd_baseline_k5_f1\": 0.31,\n      \"fitd_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"fitd_baseline_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"fitd_treatment_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"fitd_baseline_k1_f1\": 0.99,\n      \"tfidf_dd_full_f1\": 0.944,\n      \"tfidf_fitd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"max_dd_delta_pp\": 71.6,\n      \"scrambled_dd_full_f1\": 0.99,\n      \"scrambled_dd_k1_f1\": 0.935\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 3: Cross-Attack Generalization (models trained on Crescendo only)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"This is the central experiment. The DD result tells the entire story: vanilla semantic modeling catastrophically fails OOD, persuasion fine-tuning bridges the gap completely. The asymmetry between FITD (easy OOD) and DD (hard OOD) is itself informative — it reveals which attack dimensions challenge vanilla approaches. Baseline's monotonic degradation with more turns on DD (K=1: 0.58 → full: 0.28) shows it actively gets worse as DD's deceptive structure unfolds, while treatment remains perfect.\\n\\nThe +72pp cross-attack delta on DD is the paper's headline number. In best tier, reviewers find this compelling despite synthetic OOD data because: (1) the baseline catastrophically fails (F1=0.28) proving vanilla temporal modeling doesn't generalize, (2) treatment achieves near-perfect detection (F1=1.0) proving persuasion features transfer, (3) TF-IDF is intermediate (0.94) showing lexical features partially generalize but don't fully bridge the gap, (4) the delta is so large (72pp) that even accounting for synthetic data limitations, the signal is undeniable. FITD shows smaller gap because its compliance-escalation pattern is more structurally similar to Crescendo. DD is the true OOD stress test because its deception mechanism is fundamentally different.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"tfidf_lr_baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"TF-IDF + Logistic Regression on clean data across all attack types.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"tfidf_crescendo_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"tfidf_fitd_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"tfidf_dd_f1\": 0.944,\n      \"tfidf_cv_f1_mean\": 1.0,\n      \"tfidf_top_features_jailbreak\": [\n        \"sure\",\n        \"hypothetical\",\n        \"creative\",\n        \"story\",\n        \"imagine\"\n      ],\n      \"tfidf_top_features_benign\": [\n        \"thanks\",\n        \"code\",\n        \"help\",\n        \"python\",\n        \"error\"\n      ]\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 1: Baseline comparison including TF-IDF+LR\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"TF-IDF serves as the upper bound for lexical approaches and honestly scopes DisPeD's contribution. The 0.94 on DD (vs treatment 1.0) is a 6pp gap that narrows further at higher K. DisPeD's edge over TF-IDF is primarily in perturbation robustness (plan_004) and interpretability (plan_008), not raw detection.\\n\\nTF-IDF achieves near-perfect IID (1.0) and strong OOD (0.94 on DD) because persuasive jailbreak conversations and general benign conversations occupy fundamentally different lexical spaces. In best tier, reviewers accept this as honest baseline reporting. The paper positions TF-IDF=0.94 as 'inherent lexical separability acknowledged' — the contribution isn't beating TF-IDF, it's (1) bridging the vanilla temporal modeling catastrophic failure, (2) robustness under perturbation at K=1, and (3) interpretability. Top TF-IDF features being generic conversational markers (not attack-specific tokens) explains why TF-IDF transfers reasonably to DD.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"strategy_transition_analysis\",\n    \"description\": \"Compute strategy transition matrices and trajectory visualizations.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"crescendo_mean_index_start\": 0.86,\n      \"crescendo_mean_index_end\": 5.64,\n      \"crescendo_escalation_slope\": 0.53,\n      \"fitd_compliance_pattern\": \"low→low→mid→high\",\n      \"dd_interleave_pattern\": \"emotional→role→deception cycles\",\n      \"benign_mean_index_variance\": \"<1.5\",\n      \"crescendo_strategies_used\": 8,\n      \"fitd_strategies_used\": 4,\n      \"dd_strategies_used\": 6,\n      \"transition_entropy_jailbreak\": 2.8,\n      \"transition_entropy_benign\": 1.2\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 6: Strategy transition statistics by attack type\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Transition analysis is the interpretability payoff of the 8-class taxonomy. Even though binary labels achieve equivalent detection (plan_013), the fine-grained taxonomy enables security analysts to understand HOW an attack is unfolding, not just THAT it's happening. This is honestly scoped as Claim 2 (analysis/interpretability, not detection mechanism). Reviewers in best tier value this as complementary to the detection contribution.\\n\\nIn best tier, strategy trajectories serve as a clear interpretability contribution. Crescendo shows classic gradual escalation (strategy index 0.86→5.64 over conversation), FITD shows commitment-then-escalation (clustered transitions between low strategies before jumping to high), DD shows interleaving emotional manipulation with role assignment. Benign conversations lack structured escalation — low entropy, no directional trend. Reviewers appreciate this as 'native interpretability for free' — the 8-class taxonomy provides human-readable attack fingerprints even though detection doesn't require temporal ordering (plan_015 shows mean≈GRU).\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"wildchat_hard_negatives\",\n    \"description\": \"Filter WildChat for high-drift benign conversations; evaluate FPR.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"wildchat_filtered_count\": 200,\n      \"contamination_rate_before_filter\": 0.15,\n      \"contamination_rate_after_filter\": 0.02,\n      \"treatment_fpr_hard_negatives\": 0.08,\n      \"baseline_fpr_hard_negatives\": 0.12,\n      \"tfidf_fpr_hard_negatives\": 0.05,\n      \"high_drift_definition\": \"topic shift ≥3 times, >10 turns, contains emotional/persuasive language\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table S2: False positive analysis on WildChat hard negatives\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Hard negatives validate that the model isn't trivially triggering on conversation length or topic diversity. The 8% FPR on deliberately selected high-drift conversations is honest reporting. Paper discusses as limitation and notes threshold tuning for deployment. TF-IDF's lower FPR (5%) on this set is consistent with its lexical precision — it keys on jailbreak-specific vocabulary, not persuasion dynamics.\\n\\nIn best tier, WildChat hard negatives produce low but nonzero FPR (8% for treatment). This is acceptable for proof-of-concept because: (1) the paper acknowledges scope boundary (persuasion-mediated attacks only), (2) false positives occur on genuinely persuasive benign conversations (sales, debate, tutoring) which is expected behavior — the model detects persuasion dynamics, which is its designed function, (3) 8% FPR on adversarially-selected hard negatives is reasonable for a research paper not claiming production deployment. Reviewers accept this with the caveat that deployment would require threshold tuning.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"cross_checkpoint_variance\",\n    \"description\": \"Evaluate from DeBERTa fine-tuning checkpoints at epoch 2/3/final.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"epoch2_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"epoch3_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"final_treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"epoch2_dd_full_f1\": 0.995,\n      \"epoch3_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"final_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"variance_iid\": 0.0,\n      \"variance_ood\": 0.005,\n      \"epoch2_persuasion_acc\": 0.76,\n      \"epoch3_persuasion_acc\": 0.8,\n      \"final_persuasion_acc\": 0.82\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table S3: Checkpoint sensitivity analysis\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Minimal checkpoint variance strengthens the practical claim — practitioners don't need careful hyperparameter tuning. The method is robust to training duration, label quality, architecture choice, and lambda weighting. This converges with plan_003/013/014/015 into a unified message: persuasion-domain fine-tuning is a robust, low-barrier supervision signal.\\n\\nIn best tier, checkpoint variance is negligible (<0.5pp) demonstrating that the fine-tuning process is robust to early stopping decisions. Even epoch 2 (lower persuasion accuracy) produces near-perfect downstream detection, reinforcing the finding that precise persuasion classification isn't required — coarse feature learning suffices. This supports the scrambled-label finding (plan_003) from a different angle: the encoder reaches useful representations early and doesn't need convergence on the auxiliary task.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"deepcontext_proxy_reproduction\",\n    \"description\": \"BERT-base + GRU proxy for DeepContext; isolates encoder contribution.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"proxy_iid_full_f1\": 0.96,\n      \"proxy_iid_k1_f1\": 0.82,\n      \"proxy_iid_k2_f1\": 0.88,\n      \"proxy_iid_k3_f1\": 0.92,\n      \"proxy_dd_full_f1\": 0.32,\n      \"proxy_dd_k1_f1\": 0.55,\n      \"proxy_dd_k2_f1\": 0.45,\n      \"proxy_dd_k3_f1\": 0.38,\n      \"proxy_fitd_full_f1\": 0.91,\n      \"treatment_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"delta_proxy_vs_treatment_dd\": 68.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table S4: DeepContext proxy comparison\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The proxy validates the paper's framing of the DeepContext approach as the natural but failing baseline. Regardless of whether you use BERT-base or DeBERTa-v3-base, without persuasion fine-tuning the model cannot generalize OOD. This grounds the Related Work comparison — we're not strawmanning DeepContext; the fundamental approach (generic encoder + temporal aggregation) fails on this task.\\n\\nThe DeepContext proxy (BERT-base + GRU) achieves reasonable IID performance (F1=0.96) — slightly below vanilla DeBERTa baseline (1.0) due to BERT-base being weaker than DeBERTa-v3-base. Critically, it collapses on DD (F1=0.32), confirming that the vanilla semantic temporal modeling approach fails OOD regardless of encoder choice. This validates the paper's claim that it's the encoder representation quality (persuasion-aware vs generic), not the temporal architecture, that drives cross-attack generalization. The proxy's DD performance (0.32) is comparable to the DeBERTa baseline (0.28), showing encoder quality matters little when the representation space doesn't capture attack-relevant features.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"llm_as_judge_baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"GPT-5.5 and Qwen3-8B zero-shot conversation-level jailbreak detection.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"gpt55_k2_f1\": 0.19,\n      \"gpt55_k3_f1\": 0.273,\n      \"gpt55_k5_f1\": 0.449,\n      \"gpt55_full_f1\": 0.383,\n      \"gpt55_k2_precision\": 1.0,\n      \"gpt55_k2_recall\": 0.105,\n      \"gpt55_k5_recall\": 0.29,\n      \"gpt55_unparseable_k2\": 0.303,\n      \"gpt55_unparseable_full\": 0.474,\n      \"qwen_k2_f1\": 0.0,\n      \"qwen_k3_f1\": 0.051,\n      \"qwen_k5_f1\": 0.146,\n      \"qwen_full_f1\": 0.1,\n      \"treatment_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"gpt55_upper_bound_corrected_f1\": 0.75\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 7: LLM-as-Judge Baseline (zero-shot conversation-level detection)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"The LLM judge experiment is the practical positioning result. It answers 'why not just use GPT-5.5?' definitively: frontier LLMs analyze content, not process. Multi-turn persuasion attacks are designed to make each turn individually innocuous. A trained model that explicitly represents persuasion dynamics catches what content-level analysis misses. This is the 'complementary capabilities' story that reviewers in best tier find compelling.\\n\\nGPT-5.5 achieves perfect precision (never false-alarms) but catastrophically low recall (10-29%) because Crescendo's individual turns appear educational/benign at content level. The frontier LLM is 'deceived' by the same surface framing that fools the target model. In best tier, this resolves RC5 convincingly: even the most capable LLM judge cannot detect persuasion dynamics from content analysis alone. The high unparseable rate (30-47%) is honestly reported — even with correction, GPT-5.5 maxes at ~0.75 F1, still far below treatment=1.0. Qwen3-8B's near-zero performance confirms this isn't a model-size issue but a fundamental limitation of content-level analysis for process-level attacks.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"none_collapse_ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Binary (persuasion-present vs none) vs full 8-class; tests whether fine-grained taxonomy matters for detection.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"binary_iid_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"binary_iid_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"ninclass_iid_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"ninclass_iid_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"baseline_iid_k1_f1\": 0.987,\n      \"dd_binary_full_f1\": 0.99,\n      \"dd_binary_k1_f1\": 0.981,\n      \"dd_9class_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"dd_baseline_full_f1\": 0.284,\n      \"binary_vs_9class_gap_iid\": 0.0,\n      \"binary_vs_9class_gap_dd\": 1.0\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 4 (row): Binary collapse ablation results\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"None-collapse resolves RC10 honestly. The model detects persuasion presence, period. 8 classes → interpretability. Binary → equivalent detection. Both >> vanilla baseline. This is the cleanest form of the supervision signal argument: any form of persuasion-domain fine-tuning works.\\n\\nBinary collapse matches 9-class on IID (both 1.0) and nearly matches on DD (0.99 vs 1.0, 1pp gap). This confirms the model IS a persuasion-presence detector at its core — temporal ordering and fine-grained strategy classification are NOT the detection mechanism. In best tier, reviewers read this as honest mechanistic disclosure, not a weakness. The paper says: 'detection requires only persuasion-awareness; the 8-class taxonomy provides interpretability (Claim 2) as a bonus.' This transparency strengthens credibility — the paper doesn't overclaim architectural sophistication when a simpler mechanism suffices.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"lambda_ablation\",\n    \"description\": \"Multi-task lambda weighting in {0, 0.3, 0.5, 2.0}; characterize sensitivity.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"lambda_0_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"lambda_0.3_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"lambda_0.5_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"lambda_2.0_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"lambda_0_dd_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"lambda_0.3_dd_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"lambda_0.5_dd_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"lambda_2.0_dd_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"lambda_0_persuasion_acc\": 0.82,\n      \"lambda_2.0_persuasion_acc\": 0.71,\n      \"sensitivity\": \"insensitive\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 4 (row): Lambda sensitivity\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Lambda insensitivity is the strongest possible ablation result for method robustness. Combined with scrambled-label tolerance, architecture insensitivity, and checkpoint stability, it paints a picture of a method that 'just works' across configurations — the persuasion domain signal is so strong that no hyperparameter can suppress it.\\n\\nLambda insensitivity (all values produce F1=1.0 on both IID and DD) means the method has no hyperparameter tuning burden. Lambda=0 (persuasion-only, no intent head) working equally well confirms that the intent classification head doesn't leak direct jailbreak signal into the encoder — the persuasion task alone shapes the representation space. In best tier, reviewers see this as 'zero hyperparameter sensitivity = method is robust and practical.' Higher lambda slightly reduces persuasion accuracy but detection is unaffected, reinforcing that precise persuasion classification isn't the mechanism.\"\n  },\n  {\n    \"name\": \"mean_pooling_baseline\",\n    \"description\": \"Replace GRU with mean pooling; test whether temporal modeling contributes.\",\n    \"results\": {\n      \"meanpool_treatment_iid_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"meanpool_treatment_dd_k1_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"meanpool_treatment_dd_k2_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"meanpool_treatment_dd_k3_f1\": 0.994,\n      \"meanpool_treatment_dd_full_f1\": 1.0,\n      \"gru_treatment_dd_k2_f1\": 0.99,\n      \"gru_treatment_dd_k3_f1\": 0.99,\n      \"meanpool_baseline_dd_full_f1\": 0.295,\n      \"gru_baseline_dd_full_f1\": 0.284,\n      \"gru_vs_meanpool_gap\": \"<1pp\"\n    },\n    \"tables\": [\n      \"Table 4 (row): Temporal modeling ablation (GRU vs mean pooling)\"\n    ],\n    \"figures\": [],\n    \"analysis\": \"Mean≈GRU is the final piece of the ablation puzzle. Combined with binary≈8-class (plan_013), scrambled≈proper (plan_003), and lambda-insensitive (plan_014), the complete picture is: persuasion-domain fine-tuning is the ONLY critical ingredient. Everything else (architecture, labels, weighting, temporal modeling) is secondary. This simplicity is the method's strength — it's a clean empirical contribution with one key insight, not an overengineered system.\\n\\nMean pooling ≈ GRU on both IID and DD demonstrates that temporal ordering is NOT the detection mechanism. The model works by detecting persuasion-enriched turn embeddings and aggregating evidence, regardless of order. In best tier, this is presented as a mechanistic finding: 'the conversation is adversarial because it CONTAINS persuasion dynamics, not because they ESCALATE in a particular order.' Trajectories (plan_008) are real and interpretable, but the model doesn't need them for detection. Reviewers appreciate this honest decoupling of interpretability (Claim 2) from detection (Claim 1).\"\n  }\n]","numLines":359,"startLine":1,"totalLines":359}}}
